#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""familiar -- the one command line: `familiar <subject> <verb> [args]` (R1l).

The subject comes first because it is how this system files everything else:
the engine's folders, the knowledge tree and the checks are all cut by the
same subjects (`config/knowledge/subjects.yaml`). A verb lives under
the subject whose code implements it, and `familiar` with no arguments prints
the whole table.

THE ROUTES ARE THE DECLARATION. Each maps `(subject, verb)` to a back-end and
the argv prefix that back-end takes. The back-ends are the three programs this
front door replaced as entry points (`cli/familiar-memory.py`,
`cli/familiar-context.py`, `structure/pazras.sh`) plus the shell helpers
(`host/pazras-utils.sh`). The name is `familiar` (naming rule 8): PAZRAS names
the folder tree, and this program is the whole system's front door. The old
`engine/pazras` is a symlink to this file until nothing external calls it.
They are not on PATH; `tests/test_front_door.py` holds every route to a verb its
back-end actually dispatches.

HOOK LATENCY. `familiar context capture` runs on every SessionEnd and
PostToolUse, so this file imports `os` and `sys` only, and runs a Python
back-end IN-PROCESS (`runpy`) rather than starting a second interpreter.
A shell back-end is `exec`'d: same process slot, no wrapper left waiting.

KEYLESS. Nothing here reads a credential; `cli/tests/test_cli_keyless.py`
scans this file with the rest.
"""

import os
